#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120918} :activity/id #uuid "c5afec26-f914-51c6-81d2-b03d5e027b77" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048648 :topic/id #uuid "b1e9fe5c-895e-5616-8f6f-1ceb3b387545" :topic/math-academy-id 1836} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120918 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121136 :tutorial/content "Recall that if the curve $\\mathbf r(s)$ is parametrized by arc length $s,$ then the curvature of $\\mathbf{r}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\kappa(s) =  \\left\\| \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf T}{\\textrm{d}s}  \\right\\|.\n$$\n\nMoreover, if $\\mathbf r(t)$ is parametrized by a general parameter $t,$ we have the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf T(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|}$ is the unit tangent vector to the curve. \n\nIt can be shown that the curvature of $\\mathbf r(t)$ can also be found using the following cross product formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this formula at the end of the lesson. For now, let's get some practice at applying it." :tutorial/id #uuid "699ec19c-b03f-5cc8-8096-613245aa4961" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7883 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3e7c32fc-b2a5-5b01-be28-7f715f065fb1" :step/math-academy-id 45439 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120919}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120919 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119947 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119517 :question/id #uuid "f0213e01-520f-5b59-96d3-1d94a50deb82" :question/math-academy-id "e-12591"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eddc00c0-eac2-5e79-84be-b90cc27eb054" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049196}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444333}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "182c4b73-5dc9-53a4-835c-6d143dc732a6" :step/math-academy-id 45443 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120923}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120920 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121137 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a plane curve $C$ that is parametrized by the vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\langle x(t), \\: y(t) \\rangle.\n$$\n\nAt first glance, it looks as though the cross product curvature formula cannot be applied since cross products apply to three-dimensional vectors, whereas the given vector $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ has only two dimensions.\n\nHowever, this curve can also be viewed as a curve in three-dimensional space that lies on the plane $z=0.$ In this case, $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ can be considered as a vector-valued function with $z$-component equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)= \\langle x(t), \\: y(t), \\: 0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nAs a result, the curvature $\\kappa$ of our plane curve $C$ can be calculated using our cross product formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t)=\\dfrac{\\|{\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t)}\\times {\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime\\prime}(t)}\\|}{\\|{\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t)}\\|^3}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2ae1ba4c-311d-5298-8526-146a426bda14" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7884 :tutorial/title "Applying the Cross Product Formula to Plane Curves"} :step/id #uuid "405daa49-6e22-58fb-86e9-6e879a856c32" :step/math-academy-id 45446 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120921}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120921 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119948 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119518 :question/id #uuid "b17659af-7eb8-5922-a8b7-ab1e9dcfda08" :question/math-academy-id "e-12592"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca07aabe-9fdd-5f28-b39f-1971c3591f86" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444331}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve"} :step/id #uuid "bf0440fe-1ba1-52ce-be45-c99625d9108a" :step/math-academy-id 45447 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120922}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120922 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121138 :tutorial/content "Recall that if the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ is parametrized by a general parameter $t,$ then the curvature of $\\mathbf{r}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf T(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|}$ is the unit tangent vector to the curve. \n\nHere, $\\mathbf r'(t)$ is a vector function and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|$ is a scalar function.\nSo, we differentiate $\\mathbf T(t)$ by using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(T) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖})\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)+\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({r}^{′}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖})\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)+\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\\cdot {r}^{″}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the cross product of a vector with itself equals $\\mathbf{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t)\\times {T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\\times (\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖})\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)+\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\\cdot {r}^{″}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{2}}\\,({r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the magnitude of both sides and use the fact that $\\mathbf T$ is a unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖T(t)\\times {T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{2}}\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ \\\\ ‖T(t)‖\\cdot ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{2}}\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ \\\\ 1\\cdot ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{2}}\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|} = \\dfrac{\\left\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\right\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f59936dc-ef8e-53d7-be8e-244d577dd658" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7885 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Cross Product Formula"} :step/id #uuid "1ca0f550-1726-5b03-9c0f-4d507624654b" :step/math-academy-id 45450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120923 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119949 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119519 :question/id #uuid "0ad622cf-852f-5795-81ec-d147f050074e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12608"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b61ddc7-b781-5a25-b499-1ea6f48abf36" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444330}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a General Point"} :step/id #uuid "390940d4-be4b-5226-9968-df737ec5e680" :step/math-academy-id 45507 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120920}}] :activity/title "Finding Curvature Using the Cross Product" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120035}]